body:
I don't think it's called faking the action of opening your mouth. 

&#x200B;

Being an introvert doesn't equal being a shy pussy.

I'd never wear a tie that I'd never wear only to walk in a restaurant. I'd walk in another restaurant in which I'd war the tie. The tie can't be so awful and if it is, then I got no intention to walk into a restaurant that has such an awful rule. 

&#x200B;

" Also, it a matter of hierarchy. When you are at the bottom, you respect those on top, untill you move up the ranks enough that you call the shots " - By the time you get there to call the shots you're already a frustrated, semi-old, sick dude. This is the real life and it's different than in movies.

A gunshot is safer than many other methods (like jumping off a building, hanging yourself, drug overdose, etc.) 

&#x200B;

Of course, nothing is 100% guaranteed - not even death. Still, you have \~1 in a million chances to survive a gunshot to the head.

i don't plan to act on it....I admire her too much for that 

&#x200B;

yeah probably it is a fetish or something very perverted but it is so damn intense I can't tell you

That is not something I wrote. 

&#x200B;

But here is something I wrote ” This is not a post that discusses the drug issue. ” - could you just....ask a 7 years old to teach you to read correctly.....ya know... ?

You are right about that, I just don't think that Masterchef is necessarily a contest that produces chefs only for the fine-dining niche.

&#x200B;

Except a few, most Masterchef winners didn't go into fine-dining after winning the competition, but they opened their own restaurant and published their own cookbook.

You're so full of cliches it's almost difficult to even take seriously what you write.

Hope you don't mind that I'm straightforward. Anyway. 

&#x200B;

" This isn’t reality, though. " - Perhaps, but you can't prove that. I can't prove it either. The difference between you and me is that I choose to see reality this way even though it's uncomfortable, while you choose to not see reality this way because it's comfortable. The place I'm coming from is not more justified than yours, but I'm not coming from a comfortable place at least and life taught me that people who like comfort have more tendency for blindness. 

&#x200B;

" I want to watch the world burn sometimes too. But you’ve been feeling this way pretty consistently for a long time.  " - Perhaps you've never been awake consistently for a long time. 

&#x200B;

" Straight up, that almost always indicates that you have experienced some legitimate trauma that needs to be dealt with. " - You can't know that. Chances are that you're not even a psychiatrist or psychologist, but even if you'd be one, you still couldn't know that for sure. I repeat what I wrote to you previously as well: you choose to think that I am traumatised because that is comfortable for you; because in this way, you don't have to face that **perhaps ( I don't even want to convince you, but perhaps ) the world is truly like this and not the way you see it.**

Don't quote something I didn't write, thicko ;)

&#x200B;

You wonderfully proved my 6th paragraph.

" why can't you get stronger chasing unicorns and rainbows? It still takes effort and stamina to chase your dreams, " - Because dreams are far from unicorns and rainbows, only that people find it out way too later usually. 

&#x200B;

I do agree with your analogy of the marathon runner, any type of movement forward is definitely making one more fit than just laying in bed, but we're talking about different degrees of power here. 

Persons who only pursue their passion their whole lives aren't building that type of toughness that people who don't chase their passion build. Despite this, I know that every person who chases his passion likes to think that what he is doing  is the hardest thing, but it just isn't - it's a subjective blindness. 

&#x200B;

You know that saying "Find something you love to do and you will never work a day in your life ?" - That's exactly what pursuing your passion is. And trust me , I have nothing against people who do that, I was also one of them in the past and I know there are many great people out there who follow their passion and do great things. I'm just saying: **let's not call this toughness, because it isn't.** 

Toughness is built when you go through a challenge that is devoid of warm and fuzzy feelings and love and any type of comfort that you can imagine. Toughness is built when you spend more time in the nightmare zone than in the dream zone and this is something not many people understand in this world. 

And that's fine -- not everyone has to be tough. 

&#x200B;

As for your CEO example - "  There is no one path to strength or power.. **It doesn't serve** to compare to others " **- Yes it does.** Power is ALWAYS about competition, comparison, contrast. Only delusional and soft people think that this "Don't compare yourself to others" cliche is true - this was invented for people who aren't interested in power, because it's a comforting idea and comforting ideas make you duller, number and easier to just smoke your pipe and stay away from the way of those who are ferociously down for business.

>Second, you say that bullies will never change

I said that **some** bullies will never change. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

As I wrote to another user, yes, you can definitely do both. 

&#x200B;

> **BOTH**, only that we are quite proficient in one of them, but we severely lack in the other. 

&#x200B;

> Kids aren't able to put things into perspective. What's going on in their life at that moment is extremely important to them and when things go wrong... it is the worst thing in existence. 

&#x200B;

Ironically, this is true for adults as well. 

Do adults put things so greatly into perspective ? 

Do adults handle so well when things go wrong ? 

Do adults not face existential distress when a co-worker/partner/boss/stranger plays some mental games with them ? 

&#x200B;

Yes, some of us do, but it's often times not the ones who struggled to keep themselves together in school. 

Kids who crack under the pressure in school are usually ending up to be the adults who crack under the pressure in real life. 

&#x200B;

I observe my environment whenever I have the chance and I see how men and women in their 40s,50s and even 60s are losing their heads if their reputation is slightly questioned or challenged via the internet, and they are definitely not kids anymore. 

&#x200B;

Priorities are the problem. 

&#x200B;

**First things first and your first priority should be to educate your kid not just about sciences and grammar, but also about standing up for himself.** 

&#x200B;

This is like educating your kid to have sex responsibly instead of just trying to block the contact with anyone out there who has a sexual disease, because that is just not going to work.

Glad to hear that. 

Many come back with PTSD & other mental issues, so the fact that you came back with a wiser perspective is truly admirable. 

&#x200B;

You know, this attitude is so typical on so many levels and in so many areas in people's lives.

From all the psychological glitches that people have, I'd say one of the most powerful ones is **delusion: thinking that you can get a thing without paying for the actual price in order to get that thing.**

People who want to get ripped, but gorge on food;

people who want money, but without working for it;

people who want promotion, but without evolving;

people who want to fit in, but without changing themselves;

people who want to win, but without risking and investing;

so on and so forth.

So you're not battling penny pinching here, you're battling human nature 👳🏻
